# Git LFS tracking for Eco Nojin large files
# Run: git lfs install && git lfs track

# ---------- Text normalisation ----------
#
# WHY this block exists, and why it is not optional.
#
# This machine (and every Windows contributor's machine) has
# `core.autocrlf = true`, so Git rewrites LF in the repository to CRLF in the
# working tree on checkout and back again on commit. Biome's formatter has no
# such setting: `formatter.lineEnding` defaults to `lf`. The two disagree, and
# the disagreement is not self-healing — an editor or a tool that writes the
# file back with CRLF produces a working-tree-only difference that Biome
# reports as a formatting error on a file nobody touched. That is exactly how
# and failed `pnpm lint` and `pnpm format:check` in CI.
#
# The failure is recurring rather than a one-off because it is a property of the
# environment, not of any one file: any future Windows checkout reproduces it.
# Fixing the one file fixes the symptom; pinning the attribute in the repository
# fixes the cause. `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine local setting that is not
# committed, so it cannot carry the guarantee — `.gitattributes` is.
#
# `text=auto` keeps Git's binary sniffing, so genuinely binary files are still
# left alone. `eol=lf` then states the working-tree ending explicitly, which
# overrides `core.autocrlf` for every path matched here. `bin/` and the LFS
# patterns below keep their own `-text`, so the ordering of the file matters:
# see the note at the end of this block.
#
# This is what makes a checkout byte-identical on Windows, macOS, and Linux,
# which is the only reason a formatter gate can be trusted in CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- LFS patterns: the rules above this line only set the repository default.
#     Every LFS-tracked extension below re-asserts `-text`, so the ordering is
#     load-bearing: `-text` on an LFS pointer keeps the pointer LF-clean and
#     keeps Git from attempting EOL conversion on a multi-gigabyte binary.
#     Do not move the normalisation block below these rules. The lines from
#     "Geospatial data" onward are unchanged from the original file.

